id: launch
name: Launch
type: standard
actionCost: 1
kiCost: 0
attacking: false
requiresTarget: false
launch: true
usageLimit: 1/round
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Throw the Character you are holding. A Grapple Check against your own Grappled;
  win and you may let go to send them flying, lose and they are the one who gets
  away.
text: >
  Launch Maneuver [1/Round]: Throw a Character you are holding.
  –Maneuver Type: Standard
  –Action Cost: 1 Action
  –KP Cost: N/A
  –Exploitable: N/A
  –Effect: Make a Grapple Check against an Opponent you are currently in a Grapple
  with as the Grappler. If you win, you may end the Grapple to move that Character
  a number of Squares up to your Might in any direction. If you lose, the Grappled
  Opponent escapes the Grapple.

# No target to pick. "An Opponent you are currently in a Grapple with as the Grappler"
# names exactly one Character, and the Grapple already knows who that is - so asking
# for a target would be asking a question with one answer, and a wrong answer to it
# would be a Launch aimed at somebody who is not being held.

---

# The Check is the Grapple's own, and the roles are the Grapple's own: the Grappler is
# the Initiator here as in every other Grapple Check, which is also what makes losing
# it release the Grappled rather than tie.
#
# What winning buys is an offer rather than something that happens. "You MAY end the
# Grapple to move that Character" - two things, and the first is the price of the
# second, so declining is a real answer and the Grapple simply stays. The offer is a
# button on the settled card, the same shape Knockback's collision uses.
#
# The throw itself is the table's, as every movement here is. What the system owes is
# the number: "a number of Squares up to your Might", read off the Grappler when the
# button is drawn rather than when the Check was opened, so it is their Might now.
